from . import T
T('7-processes','syscalls','System calls','System calls and protected kernel services',r'''
A system call is a controlled request for an operating-system service such as reading a file, creating a process or mapping memory. A user program normally cannot execute privileged device or memory-management operations directly. A library wrapper prepares arguments and enters the kernel through an architecture-defined trap/syscall mechanism, after which the kernel validates the request and returns a result or error.
A mode switch changes privilege level; a context switch changes which execution context runs. They can occur together when a call blocks, but a quick service can return to the same thread without scheduling another. Not every library function invokes a system call: a string-length calculation may run entirely in user space.
Trace whether the service can complete immediately, whether it blocks awaiting an event and which resources it alters. The kernel must treat user pointers and lengths as untrusted inputs. GATE state-transition questions may ask about a running process after a blocking read, while API questions may ask about return values. These require distinguishing the service request from the scheduler’s later decision.
''','A running process calls read on an empty blocking pipe.',r'Enter kernel::::The call requests a protected I/O service through a mode transition.~Unavailable data::::The process cannot complete the read, so it becomes blocked.~Later data::::A writer supplies data; the process becomes eligible to resume, generally ready before being scheduled.', 'The process blocks awaiting data; entering the kernel alone did not guarantee an immediate return.','A nonblocking read or a pipe already containing data would follow a different path.')
T('7-processes','process','Processes','Processes, execution states and fork/exec',r'''
A process combines a program execution with its address space, resource references and control information. The scheduler distinguishes ready, running and blocked states. Ready means able to run when assigned CPU; blocked means awaiting an event that CPU assignment alone cannot satisfy. Preemption moves running to ready, whereas waiting for I/O moves running to blocked.
Under a fork-style API, parent and child continue from the call with different return values and logically separate process memory, often implemented using copy-on-write. Exec replaces the current program image without creating an additional process. Open-resource relationships and identifiers follow the specified OS semantics rather than a blanket “everything copied” rule.
Draw a process tree and mark which branches actually reach each creation call. Unconditional successful fork calls reached by every process double the population, but conditional calls, failures and exits alter that count. Waiting for a child and replacing an image are not additional process creations. In output traces, buffering and scheduling can matter beyond simply counting the number of executing print statements.
''','One process executes two unconditional successful fork calls; no exits occur.',r'First fork::N=2::Parent and child both continue.~Second fork::N=4::Each of the two existing processes creates one child.~Interpret::::There are four total processes, including the original, not four new children.', 'Four processes: the original plus three created descendants.','The 2ᵏ rule applies because every existing process reaches both calls.')
T('7-processes','threads','Threads','Threads share resources but retain their own execution state',r'''
A thread is an execution path with its own program counter, registers and stack. Threads in one process typically share the address space, global objects and open-resource tables. This makes communication through shared variables convenient, but does not make those variables automatically safe for concurrent updates.
A process-level resource change may affect all threads, while local automatic variables usually reside in each thread’s stack. A pointer to another thread’s object can still permit access if the language and lifetime rules allow it; “separate stacks” does not create a memory-protection boundary. Scheduling and blocking behavior differ between user-level and kernel-supported thread models.
A context switch saves execution state so a suspended thread resumes at the correct instruction. Compare a process switch’s potentially different address space with a thread switch within one shared space, but avoid assuming zero cost for threads. GATE questions commonly contrast shared globals with distinct stack locals and ask which operations require synchronization. State ownership and lifetime before interpreting possible outputs.
''','Two threads each have local y and both increment global x.',r'Local instances::::Each thread has its own automatic y object.~Shared instance::::Both name the same global x in the shared process space.~Correctness::::Independent y updates need no mutual exclusion between these distinct objects; concurrent x updates can race.', 'Stacks are separate, while global x is shared.','A process-global object does not become private simply because the modifying instructions run on different stacks.')
T('7-processes','ipc','Inter-process communication','Pipes, messages and shared-memory IPC',r'''
Inter-process communication exchanges data or coordinates execution between processes. A pipe supplies a byte stream with read/write endpoints; a message queue preserves its API’s message units; shared memory maps common storage into participating address spaces. Each mechanism has different copying, buffering and blocking behavior.
Shared memory removes the need to copy every communication item through a message channel, but needs synchronization to publish consistent state. A producer must not signal readiness before finishing the data write, and a consumer must not overwrite data before it is consumed. Pipes and sockets can return partial byte counts, so application framing remains necessary for multi-byte records.
Communication does not automatically imply mutual exclusion for every external resource. Analyze buffer capacity, endpoint closure and wait conditions. EOF on a pipe can depend on all writers being closed; an empty pipe with an open writer instead can block. GATE deadlock examples may involve both parties waiting to receive before either sends, making protocol order as important as the chosen IPC facility.
''','A producer shares a buffer and a ready flag with a consumer.',r'Write payload::::The producer fills the buffer before publishing readiness.~Publish safely::::Synchronization makes completed data visible and sets ready.~Consume::::The consumer waits for readiness, reads the complete payload and acknowledges availability for reuse.', 'A synchronized publish/consume protocol is needed.','A shared address alone does not guarantee that the consumer sees a complete, current message.')
T('7-synchronization','interleavings','Concurrency;Critical section','Interleavings, races and critical-section properties',r'''
Concurrency permits operations from different executions to interleave. A race arises when correctness depends on uncontrolled relative ordering of shared accesses. A source-level increment commonly has separate read, compute and store steps, allowing lost updates. Atomicity is a guarantee for the specified operation, not a property inferred from one short source line.
A critical section accesses a shared invariant that must be protected. Mutual exclusion prevents overlapping entry, progress prevents eligible contenders from being indefinitely blocked by inactive parties, and bounded waiting limits overtaking. A safe lock can still be unfair, so these properties must be evaluated separately.
To enumerate outcomes, preserve each thread’s program order and synchronization constraints while considering legal interleavings. Under a formal language memory model a data race can make behavior undefined, so a classroom interleaving exercise needs its stated simplified model. Protect the whole read-modify-write invariant rather than only the final store. An example showing one bad interleaving proves a protocol is unsafe, while a safety proof must cover all permitted ones.
''','Shared x=0; A and B each execute x=x+1 under the simplified non-atomic model.',r'Overlapping reads::::A reads 0; B reads 0 before either writes.~Local computation::::Each computes 1 from its saved value.~Stores::::A writes 1 then B writes 1, leaving x=1.', 'Two increments can leave 1 instead of 2.','The trace respects each thread’s local order; locking the complete update prevents this lost-update interleaving.')
T('7-synchronization','locks','Synchronization;Mutex','Mutexes, condition waiting and protecting invariants',r'''
A mutex permits one owner to enter a protected region at a time. Every access that can violate the same shared invariant must follow the protocol; protecting writers but allowing unsynchronized conflicting readers is insufficient. Ownership rules commonly require the acquiring thread to release the mutex.
A condition variable waits for a predicate while associated with a lock. The wait atomically releases the lock and sleeps, then reacquires it before returning. Test the predicate in a loop because wakeups, competing consumers or permitted spurious returns can leave it false. A notification is not normally a stored permit for a future waiter.
Lock order matters when several locks are acquired. Globally consistent ordering prevents a class of circular waits, while holding a lock across a blocking operation can cause unwanted dependencies. Distinguish busy spinning from blocking; a spinlock may be unsuitable for long waits. GATE reasoning should state what resource the lock protects, what predicate a waiter needs, and which event can make progress possible.
''','A consumer waits for count>0 while protecting a shared queue.',r'Acquire mutex::::Inspect count while holding the queue lock.~Wait correctly::::While count==0, wait on the condition; the wait releases the lock so a producer can add data.~Resume and recheck::::After reacquiring the mutex, test count again before removing an item.', 'Use a while-predicate wait inside the mutex protocol.','Sleeping without releasing the queue lock could prevent the producer from ever satisfying the condition.')
T('7-synchronization','semaphores','Semaphore','Counting semaphores and producer–consumer ordering',r'''
A counting semaphore stores available permits. An atomic wait acquires a permit or blocks until one can be supplied; signal returns a permit and can wake a waiter. Some implementation descriptions allow a negative internal count to record waiters, so follow the specified convention rather than mixing models. A binary semaphore limits permits to a binary protocol but need not carry mutex ownership semantics.
For a bounded buffer, empty counts free slots, full counts produced items and a separate mutex protects structural updates. A producer waits for empty before locking the buffer, stores the item, unlocks and signals full. A consumer waits for full before locking, removes, unlocks and signals empty.
Waiting for availability while holding the mutex can deadlock: the other party needs that mutex to create availability. Initial semaphore values encode the initial state. Signals should correspond to actual resource releases or completed publications; arbitrary signaling can violate capacity invariants. Trace blocked queues and permits separately from which thread currently owns the buffer lock.
''','Three identical devices use semaphore S initialized to 3. Four clients request one each.',r'First waits::::Three successful waits consume all permits and occupy the devices.~Fourth wait::::No permit remains, so the fourth client blocks.~Release::::A holder signals after releasing its device, allowing one waiting client to proceed.', 'At most three clients hold devices simultaneously.','Initializing S to 1 would unnecessarily serialize the three devices; initializing above 3 would violate capacity.')
T('7-deadlock','conditions','Deadlock;Coffman conditions','Deadlock conditions and distinctions from starvation',r'''
Deadlock occurs when a set of executions cannot proceed because each waits for events/resources that another in the set must supply. The standard resource model needs mutual exclusion, hold-and-wait, no preemption and circular wait simultaneously. Removing a necessary condition prevents that modeled resource deadlock; its applicability depends on resource assumptions.
Starvation means one contender can be postponed indefinitely while others progress. Deadlock instead prevents progress of the involved waiting set. An unsafe allocation state lacks a guarantee of completion under maximum claims but is not necessarily currently deadlocked. These three conclusions cannot be substituted for one another.
A wait/resource graph’s directed cycle is sufficient under single-instance resource types, but multi-instance resources need more analysis. Consistent lock ordering removes circular wait; acquiring all resources at once can remove hold-and-wait but reduce concurrency. Prevention changes protocol constraints, avoidance checks prospective safety, and detection identifies existing deadlock from current requests. State which policy a proposed mechanism implements before selecting its name.
''','T1 holds A and waits for B; T2 holds B and waits for A, both exclusive nonpreemptible locks.',r'Hold-and-wait::::Each retains one lock while requesting another.~Cycle::::T1 waits for T2, and T2 waits for T1.~Progress check::::Neither can reach its release without the unavailable acquisition.', 'The two threads are deadlocked under these assumptions.','Requiring every thread to acquire A before B forbids T2’s stated holding/waiting arrangement.')
T('7-deadlock','banker','Bankers algorithm','Banker’s safety test and tentative requests',r'''
Banker’s algorithm uses declared maximum claims to avoid unsafe resource allocations. Need=Max−Allocation for each resource component. To test safety, copy Available to Work and mark all processes unfinished. Repeatedly select an unfinished process with Need≤Work componentwise, mark it hypothetically complete and add its Allocation to Work. Finishing all processes supplies a safe sequence.
Adding Allocation rather than Max is correct: resources temporarily borrowed from Work for completion are returned, leaving only the originally held allocation as net increase. If no remaining process is feasible, the state is unsafe under those claims. A sequence is a proof of possible guaranteed completion order, not necessarily the actual scheduler order.
For a new request, first reject requests above remaining Need or wait when above Available. Then tentatively update allocation/available, run safety, and commit only if safe. Maximum claims must fit the system model; the algorithm does not predict undeclared future demands. Arithmetic uses resource vectors, so a large amount of one resource cannot compensate for a shortage of another.
''','Available=1; P1 Allocation=1,Max=2; P2 Allocation=1,Max=3.',r'Needs::Need_1=1,\quad Need_2=2::Subtract current holdings from claims.~Finish P1::Work=1+1=2::Its need fits initial Work.~Finish P2::Work=2+1=3::The new Work satisfies P2’s need.', 'Safe sequence P1,P2.','Final Work equals total resources 3, confirming that no resources were accidentally created by adding maximum claims.')
T('7-scheduling','cpu','CPU scheduling;FCFS;SJF;Round robin','FCFS, shortest jobs and round-robin timelines',r'''
CPU scheduling chooses among ready processes. FCFS uses arrival order without ordinary burst preemption. Nonpreemptive SJF chooses the shortest available burst when dispatching; SRTF instead can preempt for a smaller remaining time. Neither can choose a job that has not yet arrived. Round robin assigns a time quantum and returns unfinished work to the ready queue.
Build a Gantt chart at arrivals, completions and quantum expirations. Turnaround is completion minus arrival; response is first dispatch minus arrival. For CPU-only single-burst workloads, waiting equals turnaround minus service. With I/O, blocked time needs separate accounting. Tie order and simultaneous arrivals/quantum events can change exact answers.
A small quantum improves responsiveness at the expense of frequent switch overhead; a very large one approaches FCFS. SJF’s mean-wait properties assume known burst information and specified workloads, not arbitrary future knowledge. Compute metrics per process before averaging; an average CPU burst alone does not determine an average waiting time. Include switch costs only if the question supplies them.
''','P1 burst 4, P2 burst 2, both arrive 0. Round robin quantum 2, P1 first, zero switch cost.',r'Timeline::::P1 runs 0–2, P2 runs 2–4 and finishes, then P1 runs 4–6.~Turnarounds::T_1=6,\quad T_2=4::Both arrivals were zero.~Waiting::W_1=6-4=2,\quad W_2=4-2=2::Subtract actual CPU service.', 'Mean waiting 2; mean turnaround 5.','Response times are 0 and 2, which are different from the waiting/turnaround pair.')
T('7-scheduling','disk','I/O scheduling;SCAN','Disk scheduling, direction and counted movement',r'''
For a moving-head disk model, service order determines head movement. FCFS serves requests in arrival order; SSTF chooses the nearest pending track and can starve distant requests. SCAN moves in one direction serving encountered requests, reaches the specified boundary and reverses. LOOK reverses at the last pending request instead of necessarily visiting the physical end.
C-SCAN services in one direction and wraps; C-LOOK wraps between extreme pending requests. Whether wrap or boundary motion is counted must follow the question. Direction is part of initial state, not something chosen retrospectively to improve a result. Real solid-state storage does not incur this same seek-distance model.
Sum absolute differences between consecutive visited positions, including required boundary and wrap legs. Do not replace movement by final-minus-initial displacement, which cancels reversed motion. If new requests arrive dynamically, scheduling can differ from a static queue calculation. GATE problems often distinguish SCAN from LOOK through the otherwise easy-to-forget boundary trip.
''','Head 50, requests 20,60,80, disk tracks 0…99; SCAN initially upward.',r'Upward service::::Visit 60 then 80, then continue to boundary 99.~Reverse::::After reaching 99, service remaining request 20.~Movement::(60-50)+(80-60)+(99-80)+(99-20)=128::Count every traveled segment.', '128 tracks of movement under this SCAN convention.','LOOK would reverse at 80 and total 90, so using its shorter boundary rule changes the answer.')
T('7-virtual-files','management','Memory management;Paging','Memory allocation, fragmentation and page translation',r'''
Memory management assigns physical storage and isolates access. Contiguous variable-size allocation can suffer external fragmentation: enough total free bytes exist but not in one usable run. Fixed-size paging avoids this requirement by mapping virtual pages into arbitrary frames, but can waste unused space inside the final allocated page, an internal-fragmentation effect.
A virtual address splits into page number and offset. The page table maps number to physical frame and permission/status information; the offset is preserved because page and frame sizes agree. Page-table size depends on number of virtual pages and entry width, not the amount of currently used application data alone. Multilevel tables reduce allocated metadata for sparse address spaces at the cost of more lookup stages.
Segmentation uses logical variable-sized regions with bases and bounds and has different fragmentation behavior; systems may combine mechanisms. Bounds/permission failure differs from a nonresident but valid page. GATE address calculations must state whether addresses are byte-based, the virtual address width and page size, then derive quotient/remainder or bit fields consistently.
''','Virtual address 12345, page size 4096, page 3 maps to frame 9.',r'Split::VPN=\lfloor12345/4096\rfloor=3,\quad offset=57::Quotient gives page and remainder gives byte.~Map::::Page-table entry supplies frame 9.~Recombine::PA=9(4096)+57=36921::Keep the same within-page offset.', 'Physical address 36,921.','12345=3×4096+57, and translated address has the same remainder 57.')
T('7-virtual-files','vm','Virtual memory;TLB','Demand paging, TLB misses and effective time',r'''
Virtual memory lets a process use an address space whose valid pages need not all be resident simultaneously. A valid but nonresident access raises a page fault, which may require selecting a frame, writing a dirty victim, loading the requested page and restarting. This is much slower than a memory-resident translation.
A TLB caches page translations. A TLB miss triggers a page-table lookup, but that lookup can find a resident frame without any page fault. Under a serial single-level resident model, hit cost is TLB time plus data-memory time, and miss cost adds one page-table memory access. Multilevel tables and overlaps change these paths.
Average time must weight complete mutually exclusive paths or add an explicitly extra penalty, not mix conventions. Page-fault probability can dominate even when small because disk-related service is costly. Cache hit/miss and TLB hit/miss refer to different objects: one concerns data blocks, the other translations. Inspect permissions separately, since a cached translation cannot authorize an access the mapping disallows.
''','TLB hit rate 0.9; TLB 10 ns; one-level memory access 100 ns; all pages resident.',r'Hit path::T_h=10+100=110\ ns::One translated data access.~Miss path::T_m=10+100+100=210\ ns::Read the page table then data.~Average::EAT=0.9(110)+0.1(210)=120\ ns::Weight the two complete paths.', '120 ns, with no page-fault cost in this model.','A TLB miss here adds memory lookup, not a disk fetch; including faults would need separate probability and timing.')
T('7-virtual-files','replacement','Page replacement','FIFO, LRU and optimal replacement traces',r'''
Replacement chooses a victim when a required page is absent and no free frame remains. FIFO evicts the earliest-loaded resident page; hits do not reset its load age. LRU evicts the page whose last reference is oldest and updates recency on every reference. Optimal replacement evicts the page with farthest next future use and provides an offline comparison benchmark.
Start from the stated frame contents, mark each hit/fault and update policy state after every reference. A fault can load into an empty frame without eviction but still counts. Dirty-page writeback changes service cost, not whether the reference faulted. Policies can choose different victims on the same resident set because they store different history.
FIFO can show Belady’s anomaly, where more frames lead to more faults for a particular reference string. LRU and optimal have a stack-inclusion property preventing it under their usual model. Do not assume every policy improves monotonically with capacity. GATE traces often use a hit immediately before eviction to distinguish FIFO age from LRU recency.
''','Two empty frames; references 1,2,1,3. Compare FIFO and LRU.',r'Load initial pages::::1 and 2 each fault; both policies contain {1,2}.~Hit page 1::::FIFO still regards 1 as oldest loaded; LRU now regards 2 as least recently used.~Load 3::::Both fault. FIFO evicts 1; LRU evicts 2.', 'Both have three faults but different final resident sets.','A hit changes LRU history and does not change FIFO load order.')
T('7-files','allocation','File systems;Contiguous allocation;Linked allocation;Indexed allocation','File-system blocks and allocation strategies',r'''
A file system maps logical file bytes and blocks onto storage, while managing names, metadata and free space. Contiguous allocation stores a run and locates block i arithmetically from its start; growth and finding a sufficiently long run can be difficult. Linked allocation connects blocks by pointers and supports flexible growth but needs traversal for arbitrary logical block access unless an auxiliary index is available.
Indexed allocation stores block addresses in metadata, allowing direct access through an index structure. The index itself uses storage and may need reads. Allocation strategy and directory naming are different layers: a path locates file metadata, then allocation maps requested data.
Count payload capacity separately from pointers and metadata overhead. A block containing a next pointer can have fewer user-data bytes than its physical size. Contiguity can improve locality but does not guarantee all directory operations become constant time. GATE questions comparing random access, external fragmentation and file growth need the exact representation and any cached indexing assumptions.
''','Access logical block 4 in linked allocation beginning at block 0.',r'Locate start::::File metadata identifies logical block 0’s physical location.~Follow links::::Traverse pointers for 0→1→2→3→4.~Compare contiguous::::A contiguous run could compute start+4 directly instead of reading the chain.', 'Four link traversals unless an additional cached index is available.','Logical block numbers are file positions, not necessarily consecutive physical addresses under linked allocation.')
T('7-files','inodes','Inodes','Inodes and direct/indirect capacity',r'''
An inode-style file record stores metadata and addresses for data, often mixing direct pointers with single-, double- and triple-indirect pointers. A direct pointer names one data block. A single-indirect pointer names an index block of data-block addresses; double indirection adds another index level. The directory maps a filename to a file identifier/inode, rather than necessarily storing all payload addresses itself.
With B-byte index blocks and p-byte pointers, up to floor(B/p) addresses fit after accounting for reserved metadata. Capacity for d direct plus one pointer at each of three indirect levels is B(d+k+k²+k³) data bytes under the ideal layout. Index blocks consume additional physical storage and I/O but are not user-data blocks.
Random-read cost depends on how many metadata pages are already cached and which pointer level contains the requested logical block. An inode can exist with several hard-link names; filename count need not equal object count. Follow exact pointer counts and block sizes in GATE problems instead of assuming the familiar Unix layout is universal.
''','Ten direct pointers and one single-indirect pointer; block 1024 bytes, pointer 4 bytes.',r'Index capacity::k=1024/4=256::The indirect block stores 256 addresses.~Reachable data blocks::N=10+256=266::Direct and indirect targets add.~Payload capacity::C=266(1024)=272384\ bytes::Exclude the index block’s own metadata bytes.', 'Maximum data capacity 272,384 bytes under this layout.','The single-indirect block adds one metadata block to allocated storage, not another 1024 bytes of file data.')
